# Kubernetes Certification Landscape
## Certification Levels
The target audience for associate-level certifications is beginners to the cloud and Kubernetes. Associate-level certification exams use a multiple-choice format. You will not have to interact with a Kubernetes cluster in an interactive environment.

Practitioner-level certifications are meant for developers and administrators with preexisting Kubernetes experience. Exams in this category require you to solve problems in multiple Kubernetes environments hands-on. The Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) is geared towards Kubernetes cluster administrators and does not require any other certification as a prerequisite.
## Kubestronaut Program
If you pass all Kubernetes certifications, you can call yourself a Kubestronaut. The Kubestronaut program recognized community members that we able to demonstrate their skills in the most important core areas of Kubernetes.
## Certification Overviews
### Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA)
KCNA is an entry-level certification for those interested in cloud-native application development, runtime environments, and tooling. The exam covers Kubernetes but does not require hands-on interaction with a cluster.
### Kubernetes and Cloud Native Security Associate (KCSA)
The KCSA verifies basic knowledge of security concepts and their application in a Kubernetes cluster. This exam is more advanced than the KCNA and may not be suitable for beginners.
### Certified Kubernetes Application Developer (CKAD)
The CKAD exam focuses on verifying your ability to build, configure, and deploy microservices-based applications to Kubernetes. It is suitable for developers familiar with application architecture, runtimes, and programming languages.
### Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA)
The CKA exam targets DevOps practitioners, system administrators, and site reliability engineers. It tests your ability to perform Kubernetes administration tasks, including cluster, network, storage, and beginner-level security management, with an emphasis on troubleshooting.
### Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS)
The CKS exam expands on the CKA topics, focusing on a deeper knowledge of Kubernetes security. Passing the CKA is a prerequisite for the CKS exam.
## Exam Objectives
The CKA certification program verifies a deep understanding of typical Kubernetes administration tasks, such as cluster maintenance, networking, storage solutions, and troubleshooting applications and cluster nodes.


# Kubernetes Certification Landscape

## Certification Levels

The target audience for associate-level certifications is beginners to the cloud and Kubernetes. Associate-level certification exams use a multiple-choice format. You will not have to interact with a Kubernetes cluster in an interactive environment.

Practitioner-level certifications are meant for developers and administrators with preexisting Kubernetes experience. Exams in this category require you to solve problems in multiple Kubernetes environments hands-on. The Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) is geared towards Kubernetes cluster administrators and does not require any other certification as a prerequisite.

## Kubestronaut Program

If you pass all Kubernetes certifications, you can call yourself a Kubestronaut. The Kubestronaut program recognized community members that we able to demonstrate their skills in the most important core areas of Kubernetes.

## Certification Overviews

### Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA)
KCNA is an entry-level certification for those interested in cloud-native application development, runtime environments, and tooling. The exam covers Kubernetes but does not require hands-on interaction with a cluster.

### Kubernetes and Cloud Native Security Associate (KCSA)
The KCSA verifies basic knowledge of security concepts and their application in a Kubernetes cluster. This exam is more advanced than the KCNA and may not be suitable for beginners.

### Certified Kubernetes Application Developer (CKAD)
The CKAD exam focuses on verifying your ability to build, configure, and deploy microservices-based applications to Kubernetes. It is suitable for developers familiar with application architecture, runtimes, and programming languages.

### Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA)
The CKA exam targets DevOps practitioners, system administrators, and site reliability engineers. It tests your ability to perform Kubernetes administration tasks, including cluster, network, storage, and beginner-level security management, with an emphasis on troubleshooting.

### Certified Kubernetes Security Specialist (CKS)
The CKS exam expands on the CKA topics, focusing on a deeper knowledge of Kubernetes security. Passing the CKA is a prerequisite for the CKS exam.
## Exam Objectives
The CKA certification program verifies a deep understanding of typical Kubernetes administration tasks, such as cluster maintenance, networking, storage solutions, and troubleshooting applications and cluster nodes.
# Curriculum
The following overview lists the high-level sections, or domains, of the exam and their scoring weights:
- 25%: Cluster Architecture, Installation and Configuration
- 15%: Workloads and Scheduling
- 20%: Servicing and Networking
- 10%: Storage
- 30%: Troubleshooting
## Kubernetes Cluster Management
### Cluster Architecture, Installation and Configuration
This section covers the fundamental architecture of Kubernetes, including the control plane and worker nodes, high-availability configurations, and tools for installing, upgrading, and maintaining a cluster. You'll also explore extension interfaces, installing a cluster from scratch, upgrading versions, and backing up/restoring etcd.

Key topics include:
- Kubernetes architecture and components
- Cluster installation and configuration
- Cluster upgrades and backups
- RBAC and cluster access management
- Kubernetes operators and deployment tools
### Workloads and Scheduling
This domain focuses on managing cloud-native applications in Kubernetes. It covers key resources like Deployments, ReplicaSets, and configuration management using ConfigMaps and Secrets. You'll also learn about scheduling rules, such as node affinity and taints/tolerations, to ensure optimal workload placement.
### Servicing and Networking
This domain evaluates your knowledge of Kubernetes networking primitives, including Services, Ingress, NetworkPolicy, and the Gateway API. Mastering these components is crucial for managing communication within and outside the cluster.
### Storage
This domain covers the various types of volumes used for reading and writing data in Kubernetes. You'll need to demonstrate the ability to manage Persistent Volumes, understand static and dynamic provisioning, and configure storage resources efficiently.
### Troubleshooting
This domain carries the highest scoring weight in the exam, as it tests your ability to diagnose and resolve issues in production Kubernetes clusters. Developing effective troubleshooting strategies is critical to maintain the stability and reliability of Kubernetes environments.
By mastering these key areas, you'll be well-prepared to take on the role of a Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA).

# Involved Kubernetes Primitives
Some of the exam objectives can be covered by understanding the relevant core Kubernetes primitives. Be aware that the exam combines multiple concepts in a single problem. Refer to [Figure 1-2](https://learning.oreilly.com/library/view/certified-kubernetes-administrator/9798341608399/ch01.html#kubernetes-primitives) as a guide to the applicable Kubernetes resources and their relationships.
